Based on my analysis of the radiograph — zooming into each region, enhancing local contrast, and mapping coordinates against a grid — here is my best assessment:

The most consistent radiolucencies I can identify are in the mandible: the periapical bone around the right molar (grossly carious crown) and the adjacent restored/carious premolar region, plus the apex of the left restored molar. The darker area above the maxillary right molar is inseparable from the maxillary sinus, so I did not score it as a lesion.

```json
{"lesions": [
  {"box": [0.28, 0.59, 0.34, 0.67], "pai": 3},
  {"box": [0.34, 0.585, 0.40, 0.67], "pai": 4},
  {"box": [0.60, 0.61, 0.66, 0.70], "pai": 3}
]}
```

Caveats worth stating plainly: this is a film panoramic with moderate resolution and trabecular noise, so periapical changes are subtle and my localizations carry uncertainty. The box for the left molar is the weakest of the three; if you have the dataset ground-truth or a higher-resolution periapical series, those would let you confirm or discard it.
